[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Context API vs Stores in Svelte: A Technical Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When managing state in Svelte applications, developers often face a choice between Svelte Stores and the native Context API. While both provide mechanisms for sharing data across component trees without prop drilling, they serve fundamentally different use cases and architectural patterns."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Svelte Stores"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte stores are reactive objects that hold state and can be accessed from anywhere in your application. They are globally scoped by default, making them ideal for application-wide state management such as user authentication, themes, or global shopping carts."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import { writable } from "svelte/store";

export const userStore = writable({
  name: "John Doe",
  isAuthenticated: true
});"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Context API"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Context API, provided by 'setContext' and 'getContext', allows you to pass data down the component tree to deeply nested children without passing props at every level. Context is scoped to the component subtree where it is defined, preventing global pollution."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import { setContext } from "svelte";
import { writable } from "svelte/store";

const themeStore = writable("dark");
setContext("theme", themeStore);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: Stores are globally accessible, whereas Context is scoped to a specific component tree."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reactivity: Stores are inherently reactive primitives. Context can pass any JavaScript data, including stores, but the context itself is set during component initialization."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle: Stores exist for the lifetime of the application or until destroyed. Context lives as long as the provider component is mounted."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Which"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use Svelte Stores when you need global state that persists across page layouts or needs to be accessed by unrelated components. Use the Context API when building component libraries, encapsulating component-specific state, or avoiding prop drilling for localized data."}]
